1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,632
This is a brand new Boeing 737 MAX.

2
00:00:03,632 --> 00:00:08,168
The most advanced version of the best selling airliner in history.

3
00:00:08,168 --> 00:00:11,879
This exact aircraft has six weeks left to exist.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:26,177
On the 29th of October, 2018, it will take off from Jakarta, fight its own pilots for 13 minutes, and dive into the Java Sea at nearly 800 kilometers an hour.

5
00:00:26,177 --> 00:00:27,027
189 people.

6
00:00:28,166 --> 00:00:35,632
Five months later, a second identical jet will do almost exactly the same thing over Ethiopia.

7
00:00:35,632 --> 00:00:38,432
Six minutes after takeoff. 157 more.

8
00:00:39,566 --> 00:00:44,602
346 people, killed by the same aircraft, in the same way, twice.

9
00:00:44,602 --> 00:00:50,897
And here is the part that should frighten you. It was not a freak accident.

10
00:00:50,897 --> 00:00:52,575
It was a decision.

11
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:59,652
Both planes were brought down by a piece of software the pilots had never heard of.

12
00:00:59,652 --> 00:01:02,628
Because Boeing had it deleted from the manuals.

13
00:01:02,628 --> 00:01:05,977
Most 737 MAX pilots did not know it existed.

14
00:01:07,099 --> 00:01:12,230
It read the world through a single sensor the size of a small blade.

15
00:01:12,230 --> 00:01:18,093
When that one sensor lied, the software pushed the nose of the plane toward the ground.

16
00:01:18,093 --> 00:01:19,925
Again, and again, and again.

17
00:01:21,066 --> 00:01:24,231
This is not a story about a broken machine.

18
00:01:24,231 --> 00:01:34,075
It is a story about a company that chose speed over safety, hid what it had done from its own regulator, and got caught only after 346 funerals.

19
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:43,334
Boeing's own engineers, in their own messages, described the plane as designed by clowns, who were in turn supervised by monkeys.

20
00:01:43,334 --> 00:01:46,432
They were right. This is how it happened.

21
00:01:47,566 --> 00:01:50,727
How Boeing killed 346 people.

22
00:01:51,866 --> 00:01:55,105
Chapter 1. The plane Boeing didn't want to build.

23
00:01:56,233 --> 00:02:01,428
To understand why 346 people died, you have to go back to a boardroom in 2011.

24
00:02:01,428 --> 00:02:05,324
And to a threat coming from the other side of the Atlantic.

25
00:02:06,466 --> 00:02:12,375
For decades, the single aisle passenger jet market was a two horse race.

26
00:02:12,375 --> 00:02:16,466
Boeing's 737, and its European rival, the Airbus A320.

27
00:02:16,466 --> 00:02:20,102
Boeing had always kept its nose in front.

28
00:02:21,233 --> 00:02:27,776
Then, in December 2010, Airbus played a card Boeing wasn't ready for. The A320neo.

29
00:02:27,776 --> 00:02:34,320
Same trusted airframe, but with new, bigger, far more fuel efficient engines bolted on.

30
00:02:35,466 --> 00:02:40,130
For an airline, fuel is the single biggest cost after the aircraft itself.

31
00:02:40,130 --> 00:02:44,436
A jet that burns 15 percent less fuel is not a luxury.

32
00:02:44,436 --> 00:02:47,665
It is millions of dollars a year, per plane.

33
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:54,860
Orders poured in for the neo. And then came the blow that changed everything.

34
00:02:54,860 --> 00:03:00,920
In July 2011, American Airlines, a loyal Boeing customer for decades, threatened to defect.

35
00:03:02,066 --> 00:03:10,145
American placed a huge order for narrow body jets, and for the first time in a generation, a big chunk of it went to Airbus.

36
00:03:10,145 --> 00:03:13,377
Boeing was about to lose one of its biggest customers.

37
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:22,398
Boeing had been quietly studying a brand new, clean sheet aircraft to replace the aging 737 entirely.

38
00:03:22,398 --> 00:03:27,509
The right answer. The expensive answer. It would take a decade.

39
00:03:28,633 --> 00:03:31,103
Boeing did not have a decade.

40
00:03:31,103 --> 00:03:37,692
So on the 30th of August, 2011, the board made the fateful call. No new plane.

41
00:03:37,692 --> 00:03:44,280
They would strap bigger engines onto the same 737 they had been flying since the 1960s.

42
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:47,971
It would be called the 737 MAX.

43
00:03:47,971 --> 00:03:53,482
And it came with a promise to the airlines that would become its original sin.

44
00:03:53,482 --> 00:03:56,789
This plane would fly exactly like the old one.

45
00:03:57,933 --> 00:03:59,070
Same type rating.

46
00:03:59,070 --> 00:04:05,896
That meant an airline's pilots could switch to the MAX with a couple of hours on an iPad.

47
00:04:05,896 --> 00:04:10,446
No expensive simulator training. No planes sitting idle. That promise sold jets.

48
00:04:11,566 --> 00:04:23,295
Boeing believed in it so completely that, according to congressional testimony, it promised Southwest Airlines a rebate of one million dollars per aircraft if the MAX ever required simulator training.

49
00:04:24,433 --> 00:04:26,263
Hold onto that promise.

50
00:04:26,263 --> 00:04:32,670
Because everything Boeing did next, every shortcut, every silence, flows from one commercial commitment.

51
00:04:32,670 --> 00:04:36,789
Whatever happens, do not make this plane feel new.

52
00:04:37,933 --> 00:04:41,486
Chapter 2. A bigger engine, and a hidden problem.

53
00:04:42,633 --> 00:04:48,776
The whole point of the MAX was its new engines. Bigger, more efficient fans that sip fuel.

54
00:04:48,776 --> 00:04:53,474
But bigger engines create a very physical problem. They need somewhere to go.

55
00:04:54,599 --> 00:05:00,712
The original 737 was designed to sit low to the ground, back when airports loaded bags by hand.

56
00:05:00,712 --> 00:05:04,787
There simply wasn't room under the wing for these fat new engines.

57
00:05:05,933 --> 00:05:11,615
So Boeing's engineers moved the engines forward, and mounted them higher up on the wing.

58
00:05:11,615 --> 00:05:16,539
It solved the clearance problem. It also quietly changed how the aircraft flew.

59
00:05:17,666 --> 00:05:25,401
At high angles of attack, when the nose is pointed steeply upward, those large nacelles up front generate extra lift of their own.

60
00:05:25,401 --> 00:05:28,428
And that lift pushes the nose up even further.

61
00:05:29,566 --> 00:05:34,351
It was a subtle handling quirk, not a design that wanted to crash.

62
00:05:34,351 --> 00:05:42,079
But it meant the MAX, in one corner of the flight envelope, did not behave quite like every 737 before it.

63
00:05:43,199 --> 00:05:45,261
And remember the sacred promise.

64
00:05:45,261 --> 00:05:54,745
The MAX must feel identical to the old 737, or the whole no simulator deal collapses, and Boeing owes the airlines a fortune.

65
00:05:55,866 --> 00:06:00,829
So Boeing did not fix the handling with hardware. It papered over it with software.

66
00:06:00,829 --> 00:06:06,785
A quiet little program that would sense that nose up tendency and automatically push the nose back down.

67
00:06:07,933 --> 00:06:11,778
They called it MCAS. The Maneuvering Characteristics Augmentation System.

68
00:06:11,778 --> 00:06:13,915
On paper, a minor tweak.

69
00:06:13,915 --> 00:06:19,897
In reality, the most dangerous piece of software ever put on a passenger jet.

70
00:06:21,033 --> 00:06:23,306
Chapter 3. One sensor.

71
00:06:24,433 --> 00:06:26,451
Here is what MCAS actually did.

72
00:06:26,451 --> 00:06:35,195
When it thought the nose was too high, it reached into the tail of the aircraft and moved the horizontal stabilizer, forcing the whole nose down.

73
00:06:36,333 --> 00:06:40,888
That is an enormous amount of control authority to hand to an automatic system.

74
00:06:40,888 --> 00:06:45,769
And to decide when to fire, MCAS needed to know the plane's angle of attack.

75
00:06:45,769 --> 00:06:47,722
How steeply the nose was pointed.

76
00:06:48,866 --> 00:06:54,453
A 737 has two angle of attack sensors, one on each side of the nose.

77
00:06:54,453 --> 00:07:00,412
Two, so that if one fails, the other can catch the lie. Basic, life saving redundancy.

78
00:07:01,533 --> 00:07:07,419
MCAS, as Boeing designed it, listened to only one of them at a time. Not both.

79
00:07:07,419 --> 00:07:09,258
Not a comparison. One vane.

80
00:07:09,258 --> 00:07:14,777
One point of failure, feeding a system that could push the nose at the ground.

81
00:07:15,899 --> 00:07:22,393
A former Boeing engineer would later say that relying on a single sensor for a hazard like this should never have passed.

82
00:07:22,393 --> 00:07:27,706
If that one blade froze, or was knocked out of alignment, MCAS would be acting on a lie.

83
00:07:28,833 --> 00:07:30,092
It got worse.

84
00:07:30,092 --> 00:07:38,066
In the original safety analysis, MCAS could nudge the stabilizer by about zero point six degrees at a time.

85
00:07:38,066 --> 00:07:41,424
A gentle correction. That is what regulators reviewed.

86
00:07:42,566 --> 00:07:46,470
But during flight testing, Boeing found the plane needed more.

87
00:07:46,470 --> 00:07:52,327
So it increased MCAS's power to 2.5 degrees per activation. More than four times stronger.

88
00:07:52,327 --> 00:07:57,012
And that change was not put back through the full safety review.

89
00:07:58,133 --> 00:08:00,905
And it could do it over and over.

90
00:08:00,905 --> 00:08:08,874
Every time a pilot corrected the trim, MCAS, still fed by that one bad sensor, would wait a few seconds and fire again.

91
00:08:08,874 --> 00:08:11,299
Stacking nose down commands toward the limit.

92
00:08:12,433 --> 00:08:15,070
So picture the trap Boeing had built.

93
00:08:15,070 --> 00:08:24,867
A single wrong reading, from a single unguarded sensor, could trigger a hidden system, at four times its reviewed strength, that shoves the nose down relentlessly.

94
00:08:25,999 --> 00:08:30,405
Any pilot could beat it, in theory, if they knew it was there.

95
00:08:30,405 --> 00:08:34,471
Flip two switches, cut the power to the trim, and MCAS dies.

96
00:08:34,471 --> 00:08:38,198
But that only works if you know MCAS exists at all.

97
00:08:39,333 --> 00:08:42,102
Chapter 4. Deleted from the manual.

98
00:08:43,233 --> 00:08:46,033
This is where the engineering failure becomes something uglier.

99
00:08:46,033 --> 00:08:51,010
Because Boeing made a choice about what to tell the people who would fly the plane.

100
00:08:51,010 --> 00:08:53,499
And the people who had to approve it.

101
00:08:54,633 --> 00:09:00,175
If pilots had to learn a whole new automatic system, regulators might demand simulator training.

102
00:09:00,175 --> 00:09:05,347
That would break the sacred promise, and cost Boeing that million dollars a plane.

103
00:09:05,347 --> 00:09:07,564
So MCAS had to stay invisible.

104
00:09:08,700 --> 00:09:11,304
At Boeing's request, according to the U.S.

105
00:09:11,304 --> 00:09:15,768
House investigation, references to MCAS were removed from the flight crew manuals.

106
00:09:15,768 --> 00:09:20,976
The one system that could kill them, edited out of the pilots' own books.

107
00:09:22,100 --> 00:09:28,684
The man managing Boeing's dealings with the regulator on pilot training was chief technical pilot Mark Forkner.

108
00:09:28,684 --> 00:09:35,657
In 2016, testing MCAS in a simulator, he wrote to a colleague that it was, quote, running rampant.

109
00:09:36,800 --> 00:09:41,701
Granted, he added, I suck at flying. But even this was egregious.

110
00:09:41,701 --> 00:09:50,278
Messages that would, years later, be read aloud to the world as evidence of how much Boeing knew, and how early.

111
00:09:51,400 --> 00:09:59,171
In the same exchange, Forkner talked about having, in effect, jedi mind tricked the regulators into accepting less pilot training.

112
00:09:59,171 --> 00:10:03,834
It is one of the most damning sentences in modern corporate history.

113
00:10:04,966 --> 00:10:08,980
One crucial caveat, and we will hold Boeing to the truth here.

114
00:10:08,980 --> 00:10:18,680
Forkner's defense was that he was describing a glitchy simulator, not the real plane, and that Boeing's own engineers had kept him in the dark about MCAS's expanded power.

115
00:10:19,800 --> 00:10:25,521
A jury would later agree, and acquit him. Which raises a darker question.

116
00:10:25,521 --> 00:10:34,324
If the man handling the regulator didn't fully understand MCAS either, then who, exactly, was in control of this airplane?

117
00:10:35,466 --> 00:10:38,308
And how did this get approved at all?

118
00:10:38,308 --> 00:10:41,506
Because of an arrangement between Boeing and the FAA.

119
00:10:41,506 --> 00:10:47,900
Under a program called ODA, Boeing employees were deputized to certify Boeing's own aircraft on the regulator's behalf.

120
00:10:49,033 --> 00:10:57,914
The House committee found this created inherent conflicts of interest, and that Boeing repeatedly failed to pass critical information about MCAS up to the FAA.

121
00:10:57,914 --> 00:11:01,467
The watchdog was, in part, the company it was watching.

122
00:11:02,600 --> 00:11:08,289
So the plane went into service. Hidden system. Single sensor. Quadrupled power.

123
00:11:08,289 --> 00:11:13,979
No mention in the manual. Pilots who had never heard its name.

124
00:11:13,979 --> 00:11:18,247
Every safety net, quietly cut, to protect a promise.

125
00:11:19,366 --> 00:11:27,387
By 2018, more than 200 of these aircraft were in the sky, carrying tens of thousands of people a day.

126
00:11:27,387 --> 00:11:33,002
The trap was set, and boarding passengers. All it needed was one bad sensor.

127
00:11:34,133 --> 00:11:37,215
Chapter 5. Lion Air 610.

128
00:11:38,333 --> 00:11:44,457
Jakarta, Indonesia. The aircraft is the one we met at the start.

129
00:11:44,457 --> 00:11:52,622
A nearly new 737 MAX 8, registration PK-LQP, flown by the low cost carrier Lion Air.

130
00:11:53,766 --> 00:11:58,016
Its left angle of attack sensor had recently been replaced.

131
00:11:58,016 --> 00:12:03,115
The replacement, later traced to a repair shop in Florida, was miscalibrated.

132
00:12:03,115 --> 00:12:06,514
It read about 21 degrees too high. Permanently.

133
00:12:07,633 --> 00:12:10,694
Here is the fact that should have grounded the plane.

134
00:12:10,694 --> 00:12:17,429
The day before the crash, the very same aircraft, with the very same fault, nearly went down on a flight to Jakarta.

135
00:12:18,566 --> 00:12:23,356
MCAS activated on that flight too, shoving the nose down over and over.

136
00:12:23,356 --> 00:12:29,250
That crew was baffled. They were saved by luck, in the form of a third pilot.

137
00:12:30,400 --> 00:12:35,137
An off duty pilot happened to be riding in the jump seat, watching.

138
00:12:35,137 --> 00:12:40,967
He recognized the runaway trim, and told the crew to cut the power to the stabilizer.

139
00:12:40,967 --> 00:12:44,611
The exact switches that kill MCAS. The plane landed safely.

140
00:12:45,733 --> 00:12:49,373
A plane had nearly killed a full load of passengers.

141
00:12:49,373 --> 00:12:54,832
It was written up, cleared, and sent back out to fly again the next morning.

142
00:12:54,832 --> 00:12:56,652
The trap reset itself overnight.

143
00:12:57,800 --> 00:13:01,291
October 29, 2018. 6:20 in the morning.

144
00:13:01,291 --> 00:13:09,271
Flight 610 lifts off from Jakarta, bound for the island of Bangka. 189 people on board.

145
00:13:09,271 --> 00:13:13,761
Two pilots who have never heard the word MCAS.

146
00:13:14,900 --> 00:13:18,028
The moment the flaps come up, MCAS wakes.

147
00:13:18,028 --> 00:13:24,284
Fed by that one broken sensor, it believes the nose is dangerously high. It is not.

148
00:13:24,284 --> 00:13:27,413
And it begins to push the nose down.

149
00:13:28,533 --> 00:13:32,237
The captain pulls back. MCAS pushes. He trims up.

150
00:13:32,237 --> 00:13:35,118
MCAS waits five seconds, and pushes again.

151
00:13:35,118 --> 00:13:43,762
A tug of war between a confused man and a hidden machine that will not stop, and will not explain itself.

152
00:13:44,900 --> 00:13:48,745
The flight data recorder captured the whole silent battle.

153
00:13:48,745 --> 00:13:52,590
More than 20 times, MCAS commanded the nose down.

154
00:13:52,590 --> 00:13:58,144
More than 20 times, the crew hauled it back. For over ten minutes.

155
00:13:59,266 --> 00:14:02,741
They radioed for the plane's altitude and speed to be confirmed.

156
00:14:02,741 --> 00:14:07,796
They flipped through manuals that did not contain the one word that would have saved them.

157
00:14:07,796 --> 00:14:09,375
They were fighting a ghost.

158
00:14:10,500 --> 00:14:16,755
The captain, at one point, handed control to the first officer while he searched the handbook.

159
00:14:16,755 --> 00:14:22,229
The first officer's corrections were weaker. The nose won more ground with each cycle.

160
00:14:23,366 --> 00:14:30,462
Thirteen minutes after takeoff, on the twenty somethingth push, the aircraft rolled into a dive it could not recover from.

161
00:14:30,462 --> 00:14:34,364
It hit the Java Sea at around 800 kilometers an hour.

162
00:14:35,500 --> 00:14:37,124
There were no survivors.

163
00:14:37,124 --> 00:14:45,652
189 men, women and children, killed by a system they were never told existed, reading a sensor no one had checked.

164
00:14:45,652 --> 00:14:48,901
Boeing's response would decide whether it happened again.

165
00:14:50,033 --> 00:14:52,384
Chapter 6. Five months.

166
00:14:53,533 --> 00:14:59,476
In the days after Lion Air, investigators pulled the flight data and found MCAS, fighting the crew.

167
00:14:59,476 --> 00:15:05,419
For most of the world's pilots, this was the first time they had ever heard the name.

168
00:15:06,566 --> 00:15:07,688
Pilots were furious.

169
00:15:07,688 --> 00:15:16,287
American Airlines' pilot union confronted Boeing directly, demanding to know why they had never been told about a system that could override them.

170
00:15:16,287 --> 00:15:18,530
The answers did not reassure them.

171
00:15:19,666 --> 00:15:24,207
Boeing issued a bulletin. It did not name MCAS as the villain.

172
00:15:24,207 --> 00:15:29,127
It reminded pilots of the existing procedure for runaway trim. Cut the switches.

173
00:15:29,127 --> 00:15:34,425
In effect, cope with our hidden system, using a checklist for a different problem.

174
00:15:35,566 --> 00:15:37,703
Crucially, the MAX kept flying.

175
00:15:37,703 --> 00:15:42,830
Regulators around the world, led by the FAA, did not ground it.

176
00:15:42,830 --> 00:15:47,530
Boeing's public message was steady and confident. The airplane is safe.

177
00:15:48,666 --> 00:15:56,324
But behind the confidence, Boeing's own internal analysis after Lion Air reportedly showed the risk was not a one off.

178
00:15:56,324 --> 00:16:02,067
Without a fix, another MCAS accident was a mathematical near certainty over the fleet's life.

179
00:16:03,200 --> 00:16:09,084
A software fix was coming. Boeing said so. But it slipped. Weeks became months.

180
00:16:09,084 --> 00:16:17,071
The update to make MCAS read both sensors, and lose its relentless power, was still not on the planes.

181
00:16:18,200 --> 00:16:28,204
Every day of that delay, hundreds of MAX aircraft took off with the same single sensor, the same hidden system, and now, crews who at least had a bulletin.

182
00:16:28,204 --> 00:16:29,929
It would not be enough.

183
00:16:31,066 --> 00:16:41,155
Five months and ten days after Lion Air, on the other side of the Indian Ocean, a second brand new MAX pushed its throttles forward with a full load of passengers.

184
00:16:41,155 --> 00:16:43,108
And a sensor about to fail.

185
00:16:44,233 --> 00:16:47,707
Chapter 7. Ethiopian 302.

186
00:16:48,833 --> 00:16:52,869
Addis Ababa, Ethiopia. March 10, 2019.

187
00:16:52,869 --> 00:17:00,940
This is the aircraft, registration ET-AVJ, photographed a month earlier taking off.

188
00:17:00,940 --> 00:17:07,667
Ethiopian Airlines Flight 302, bound for Nairobi. 157 people aboard.

189
00:17:08,800 --> 00:17:15,474
This crew knew more than the Lion Air pilots ever did. They had read about MCAS.

190
00:17:15,474 --> 00:17:18,811
They knew the bulletin. The captain was experienced.

191
00:17:18,811 --> 00:17:21,730
They had, in theory, everything they needed.

192
00:17:22,866 --> 00:17:26,740
Seconds after takeoff, the left angle of attack sensor failed.

193
00:17:26,740 --> 00:17:30,613
Investigators believe it may have been struck by a bird.

194
00:17:30,613 --> 00:17:36,423
Whatever the cause, it began feeding the same lethal lie. The nose is too high.

195
00:17:37,566 --> 00:17:40,562
MCAS activated, exactly as it had over Java.

196
00:17:40,562 --> 00:17:46,552
It drove the stabilizer, forcing the nose down, into the ground the aircraft had just left.

197
00:17:46,552 --> 00:17:51,045
The crew recognized it, and did what Boeing told them to do.

198
00:17:52,166 --> 00:17:56,333
They cut the electrical power to the stabilizer trim. The switches.

199
00:17:56,333 --> 00:18:00,499
The move that had saved that other flight. MCAS went silent.

200
00:18:00,499 --> 00:18:04,287
For a moment, it looked like the bulletin had worked.

201
00:18:05,433 --> 00:18:09,399
But they were trapped by physics Boeing's bulletin never explained.

202
00:18:09,399 --> 00:18:14,158
In the chaos, they had left the engines near full takeoff power.

203
00:18:14,158 --> 00:18:18,520
The plane was accelerating, faster and faster, low to the ground.

204
00:18:19,666 --> 00:18:26,406
By cutting the electric trim, they had also lost the easy way to correct the nose down MCAS had already dialed in.

205
00:18:26,406 --> 00:18:29,775
Their only option was the manual trim wheel. Turned by hand.

206
00:18:30,900 --> 00:18:32,742
And here the trap closed.

207
00:18:32,742 --> 00:18:39,744
At that speed, the aerodynamic forces on the tail were so violent that the manual wheel would barely move.

208
00:18:39,744 --> 00:18:44,536
The two pilots could not physically turn it. The plane was pointed down.

209
00:18:45,666 --> 00:18:48,984
This manual trim problem had been known about for decades.

210
00:18:48,984 --> 00:18:53,298
The technique for it had quietly been dropped from 737 training long ago.

211
00:18:53,298 --> 00:18:57,943
These pilots had never been taught the one thing that might have saved them.

212
00:18:59,066 --> 00:19:03,945
Desperate, running out of altitude, the crew made a terrible, understandable choice.

213
00:19:03,945 --> 00:19:09,636
To regain any control of the trim, they switched the electric power back on.

214
00:19:09,636 --> 00:19:12,075
Which woke MCAS one final time.

215
00:19:13,200 --> 00:19:17,463
It fired again, and drove the nose down for the last time.

216
00:19:17,463 --> 00:19:25,634
Six minutes after takeoff, Flight 302 hit a field near the town of Bishoftu at enormous speed, leaving a crater in the earth.

217
00:19:26,766 --> 00:19:30,223
157 people from 35 different countries died.

218
00:19:30,223 --> 00:19:38,124
Among them were students, aid workers, and 19 people traveling to a United Nations environmental conference.

219
00:19:38,124 --> 00:19:42,074
A second full aircraft, gone the same way.

220
00:19:43,200 --> 00:19:45,919
The two crashes were not identical.

221
00:19:45,919 --> 00:19:52,718
Investigators noted differences in how each crew responded, and the reports listed many contributing factors.

222
00:19:52,718 --> 00:19:59,971
But the spine of both was the same. One sensor. One hidden system. One relentless push.

223
00:20:01,100 --> 00:20:04,595
This time, the world did not wait for Boeing.

224
00:20:04,595 --> 00:20:10,033
Within days, one country after another slammed its airspace shut to the 737 MAX.

225
00:20:10,033 --> 00:20:15,859
And the country that had built it was, humiliatingly, one of the last to act.

226
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,194
Chapter 8. The reckoning.

227
00:20:20,333 --> 00:20:27,950
On March 13, 2019, the FAA finally grounded the 737 MAX. Every one of them, worldwide.

228
00:20:27,950 --> 00:20:35,092
It would be the longest grounding of an airliner in the history of American aviation.

229
00:20:36,233 --> 00:20:40,217
For 20 months, the world's best selling jet sat still.

230
00:20:40,217 --> 00:20:48,981
Rows of them parked in deserts and on employee car parks, wings gathering dust, worth hundreds of millions of dollars, going nowhere.

231
00:20:50,100 --> 00:20:52,962
The financial damage to Boeing was staggering.

232
00:20:52,962 --> 00:20:58,279
By the company's own accounting, the disaster cost around 20 billion dollars directly.

233
00:20:58,279 --> 00:21:03,187
Broader estimates of the total damage run to 60 billion and beyond.

234
00:21:04,333 --> 00:21:06,757
Then came the reckoning in public.

235
00:21:06,757 --> 00:21:17,264
In October 2019, Boeing's chief executive, Dennis Muilenburg, was hauled before the United States Congress to answer for the dead, on the anniversary of Lion Air.

236
00:21:18,400 --> 00:21:24,130
Behind him sat the families, holding photographs of the people they had lost.

237
00:21:24,130 --> 00:21:28,538
He was forced to confirm, out loud, the internal messages.

238
00:21:28,538 --> 00:21:32,506
Including engineers calling their own aircraft designed by clowns.

239
00:21:33,633 --> 00:21:36,663
In December 2019, Muilenburg was fired.

240
00:21:36,663 --> 00:21:44,238
He received no severance payout, and forfeited tens of millions in bonuses and unvested stock.

241
00:21:44,238 --> 00:21:50,299
A real financial penalty. But hold that word, penalty, for a second.

242
00:21:51,433 --> 00:22:02,992
Because even with no severance, Dennis Muilenburg walked away from Boeing with roughly 62 million dollars in stock and pension benefits he had already earned.

243
00:22:02,992 --> 00:22:05,304
62 million dollars. 346 dead.

244
00:22:06,433 --> 00:22:11,879
In January 2021, the United States Department of Justice settled with Boeing.

245
00:22:11,879 --> 00:22:18,234
The company was charged with one count of conspiracy to defraud the United States.

246
00:22:18,234 --> 00:22:20,957
Specifically, deceiving the FAA about MCAS.

247
00:22:22,100 --> 00:22:28,771
The total came to 2.5 billion dollars. But look at how it broke down.

248
00:22:28,771 --> 00:22:34,967
1.77 billion to compensate airline customers. 244 million as the actual criminal fine.

249
00:22:34,967 --> 00:22:39,733
And 500 million for the families of all 346 victims.

250
00:22:40,866 --> 00:22:45,722
Do that division. 500 million dollars, split among 346 lost lives.

251
00:22:45,722 --> 00:22:49,696
The families themselves pointed out what the numbers said.

252
00:22:49,696 --> 00:22:54,110
That a customer's inconvenience was priced higher than their child.

253
00:22:55,233 --> 00:22:59,548
And the question everyone asks. Did anyone go to prison?

254
00:22:59,548 --> 00:23:04,295
Not a single Boeing executive was criminally charged. The company paid.

255
00:23:04,295 --> 00:23:06,884
No leader of it was jailed.

256
00:23:08,033 --> 00:23:12,178
The only individual ever criminally charged was Mark Forkner, the technical pilot.

257
00:23:12,178 --> 00:23:20,467
And in 2022, a jury acquitted him in under two hours, accepting that he too had been left in the dark by the company.

258
00:23:21,600 --> 00:23:30,043
So the tally of individual human accountability for 346 deaths, in a court of law, came to exactly zero people.

259
00:23:30,043 --> 00:23:34,687
The corporation absorbed the blame, wrote a check, and moved on.

260
00:23:35,833 --> 00:23:40,694
And here is the epilogue that tells you whether the lesson was learned.

261
00:23:40,694 --> 00:23:47,052
In January 2024, a door plug blew off a 737 MAX 9 in mid air over Oregon.

262
00:23:47,052 --> 00:23:50,044
Bolts had been left out at the factory.

263
00:23:51,166 --> 00:23:56,320
By sheer luck, no one was sitting next to the hole, and no one died.

264
00:23:56,320 --> 00:24:02,505
But the Justice Department found the incident meant Boeing had violated the very settlement meant to reform it.

265
00:24:02,505 --> 00:24:04,567
The problem was never just MCAS.

266
00:24:05,700 --> 00:24:08,208
Chapter 9. The real cause.

267
00:24:09,333 --> 00:24:15,327
It is tempting to blame MCAS. A bad piece of software. A cheap sensor.

268
00:24:15,327 --> 00:24:20,036
But software does not have ambition. Sensors do not cut corners.

269
00:24:20,036 --> 00:24:26,887
Every fatal decision in this story was made by people, under pressure to protect a promise.

270
00:24:28,033 --> 00:24:33,559
The pressure not to trigger simulator training. The choice to hide MCAS from pilots.

271
00:24:33,559 --> 00:24:39,086
The decision to trust a single sensor. The quadrupling of its power without review.

272
00:24:39,086 --> 00:24:42,244
The five month delay while the fleet flew.

273
00:24:43,366 --> 00:24:48,789
None of those, on their own, feels like a decision to kill 346 people.

274
00:24:48,789 --> 00:24:53,438
That is exactly how these disasters work. No villain twirling a moustache.

275
00:24:53,438 --> 00:24:59,248
Just a hundred small choices, each one putting a schedule ahead of a stranger's life.

276
00:25:00,366 --> 00:25:07,835
Boeing was once an engineering company run by engineers, famous for a culture where anyone could stop the line for safety.

277
00:25:07,835 --> 00:25:11,392
Critics say that by 2018, it had become something else.

278
00:25:11,392 --> 00:25:14,237
A finance company that happened to build planes.

279
00:25:15,366 --> 00:25:20,112
The 737 MAX flies again today. It has been fixed.

280
00:25:20,112 --> 00:25:25,806
MCAS now reads both sensors, has limited power, and cannot fire relentlessly.

281
00:25:25,806 --> 00:25:32,450
The plane is, by the numbers, safe. The people it killed are still gone.

282
00:25:33,600 --> 00:25:38,841
346 empty seats. Two craters. A single sensor, and a company that chose speed.

283
00:25:38,841 --> 00:25:47,079
Remember their names the next time someone tells you that a corporation would never cut a corner that could cost a life.

284
00:25:48,200 --> 00:25:52,011
If this story got its hooks into you, subscribe to mindwired.

285
00:25:52,011 --> 00:25:58,594
We take the biggest disasters in history apart, piece by piece, to show you the decisions hiding inside them.

286
00:25:58,594 --> 00:26:01,366
Because the truth is always in the details.

